menu "DECT camera transmitter"

comment "Camera"

choice DECTCAM_FRAMESIZE
    prompt "Initial frame size"
    default DECTCAM_FRAMESIZE_QVGA
    help
      Startup resolution. Changeable at runtime with the `cam res` console
      command; this only sets the power-on default.

      The end-to-end link is limited to about 83 kB/s by the receiver's VCOM1
      USB path, so larger frames buy detail at the cost of frame rate.

config DECTCAM_FRAMESIZE_QQVGA
    bool "QQVGA 160x120  (~1-2 kB/frame)"
config DECTCAM_FRAMESIZE_QVGA
    bool "QVGA 320x240   (~4-9 kB/frame)"
config DECTCAM_FRAMESIZE_CIF
    bool "CIF 352x288    (~6-12 kB/frame)"
config DECTCAM_FRAMESIZE_VGA
    bool "VGA 640x480    (~15-35 kB/frame)"
endchoice

config DECTCAM_JPEG_QUALITY
    int "Initial JPEG quality (4-63, LOWER is better)"
    range 4 63
    default 6
    help
      OV3660 quantisation scale. This is inverted relative to the usual
      0-100 convention: 4 is near-lossless and huge, 63 is tiny and ugly.

      Measured on this link at QVGA, both with zero frame loss:
        q=12   2374 B/frame   14.6 fps   35 kB/s
        q=6    2650 B/frame   14.6 fps   39 kB/s

      q=6 is the default because it costs only 12 % more data for visibly
      cleaner edges, and the link has 57 % of its capacity spare at QVGA. The
      OV3660 compresses a static scene efficiently, so dropping q costs far
      less here than the usual rule of thumb suggests.

      Adjustable at runtime with `cam quality N`, from the transmitter's shell
      or the camera's own console.

config DECTCAM_TARGET_FPS
    int "Target frame rate"
    range 1 30
    default 15
    help
      Upper bound on capture rate. The link may not sustain it; the actual
      rate is whatever the UART drains.

config DECTCAM_XCLK_HZ
    int "Camera XCLK frequency (Hz)"
    default 20000000
    help
      The sensor derives its PLL, and hence its pixel clock, from XCLK, so this
      sets the frame rate more than anything else does. Measured on this board
      with an OV3660 at QVGA q12:

        10 MHz -> PCLK  5 MHz -> ~65 ms per frame -> 6.1 fps, 17 kB/s
        20 MHz -> PCLK 10 MHz -> ~30 ms per frame -> 13.9 fps, 38 kB/s

      Note that Freenove's own example uses 10 MHz, and that at 20 MHz the log
      shows a couple of "NO-SOI - JPEG start marker missing" lines at start-up.
      Those are the first one or two frames while the sensor settles; the
      camera driver discards them and the stream that follows is clean. They
      are not a reason to drop the clock -- during bring-up they were
      misread as one, and the real cause of the stalled stream turned out to be
      an unconnected CTS line.

config DECTCAM_VFLIP
    bool "Flip image vertically"
    default n

config DECTCAM_HMIRROR
    bool "Mirror image horizontally"
    default n

comment "Link to the nRF9151 transmitter"

config DECTCAM_UART_NUM
    int "UART port number"
    range 1 2
    default 1
    help
      UART1. UART0 is left alone so the module can still be flashed and can
      print its console log over the CH343 bridge while the data link is live.

config DECTCAM_UART_TX_GPIO
    int "UART TX GPIO (to nRF9151 RX)"
    default 42
    help
      The ESP32-S3's GPIO matrix allows any peripheral on any pin, so the
      choice is driven by what is left over -- and by which header the pin
      sits on.

      Taken by other functions: the camera (4-18), microSD (38-40), octal
      PSRAM (35-37), native USB (19/20) and the CH343 console (43/44).
      Excluded on purpose: strapping pins 0, 3, 45 and 46; the on-board LEDs
      on 2 and 48.

      GPIO42 rather than the more obvious GPIO14: on this module GPIO14 is on
      the left-hand header while CTS, RTS, RX and GND are all on the right.
      Plugged into a breadboard the module covers the left header entirely, so
      a pin there cannot be reached at all. GPIO42 is one of the JTAG pins
      (MTMS), which is free here because the S3 debugs over its native USB
      rather than these pads, and it has no boot strapping function.

config DECTCAM_UART_RX_GPIO
    int "UART RX GPIO (from nRF9151 TX)"
    default 21

config DECTCAM_UART_FLOWCTRL
    bool "Use hardware flow control"
    default y
    help
      Strongly recommended: on the equivalent VCOM link, disabling flow
      control cost 13 % of all bytes.

      Unlike the earlier AI-Thinker build, this board has a spare boot-safe
      output pin, so RTS and CTS are both wired. That matters for the nRF9151
      as well: its UARTE has a single hardware-flow-control bit covering RTS
      and CTS together, with no RTS-only mode, so the previous build had to
      fake a permanently-asserted CTS with a pull-down on an unconnected pin.
      Now the signal is real.

config DECTCAM_UART_CTS_GPIO
    int "UART CTS GPIO (from nRF9151 RTS)"
    default 41
    depends on DECTCAM_UART_FLOWCTRL
    help
      Input. The nRF asserts this when its receive buffer is filling, which
      stops us mid-stream instead of letting bytes fall on the floor.

      GPIO41 rather than the earlier GPIO1: both are free and boot-safe, but
      GPIO1 sits on row 3 of the right-hand header while TX/RX/RTS/GND sit on
      rows 5/16/17/20. On a 17-row breadboard that span cannot be covered
      without a pin hanging off the end. GPIO41 (row 6) keeps all five
      required pins inside a single 17-row window (rows 4-20) together with
      GND, with nothing unsupported. See docs/wiring.md.

config DECTCAM_UART_CTS_INVERT
    bool "Invert the CTS input"
    default n
    depends on DECTCAM_UART_FLOWCTRL
    help
      Leave this off. It exists as a documented trap, not as an option worth
      using.

      It was briefly enabled during bring-up because it appeared to fix a
      stalled link, and it does make the stream run -- but for the wrong
      reason. The `cts` console command settles it: with the link saturated
      at 15 fps, the CTS pin reads HIGH on 20000 consecutive samples with
      zero transitions, both with the inversion and without it. A genuine
      RTS signal from a receiver that is throttling would have to move.

      Nothing is driving the pin. The ESP32's internal pull-up holds it high,
      and inverting the input turns "permanently not clear to send" into
      "permanently clear to send" -- which disables flow control while
      appearing to fix it, and hides the fact that the D6 wire is not making
      contact.

      With the wire connected the polarity needs no help: the nRF asserts RTS
      low when it is ready, and the ESP32 treats CTS low as clear to send.

config DECTCAM_UART_RTS_GPIO
    int "UART RTS GPIO (to nRF9151 CTS)"
    default 47
    depends on DECTCAM_UART_FLOWCTRL
    help
      Output. Throttles the nRF's back-channel commands. Those are tiny and
      rare, so this line rarely does any work -- but it must be wired for the
      nRF's own flow control to be usable at all (see DECTCAM_UART_FLOWCTRL).

config DECTCAM_UART_BAUD
    int "UART baud rate"
    default 1000000
    help
      1 Mbaud is the nRF9151 UARTE's maximum, so it is the natural operating
      point for the pair. The ESP32-S3 could go several times faster on its
      own, but there would be nothing at the other end to receive it.

config DECTCAM_FRAG_PAYLOAD
    int "Fragment payload size (bytes)"
    range 32 1024
    default 687
    help
      Must equal the DECT transport block size minus the 13-byte protocol
      overhead, so that one fragment fits exactly one DECT PDC.

      Default 687 = 700 (MCS 4, 8 subslots) - 13.

      This MUST match CONFIG_DECTCAM_MCS / CONFIG_DECTCAM_PACKET_LEN_SUBSLOTS
      on the nRF9151 transmitter. See docs/architecture.md for
      the transport block size table.

config DECTCAM_METAINTERVAL_MS
    int "FRAME_META keepalive interval (ms)"
    default 1000
    help
      How often stream parameters are re-announced so a viewer that attaches
      mid-stream learns them quickly.

comment "Diagnostics"

config DECTCAM_STATS_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Console statistics interval (ms), 0 to disable"
    default 5000

config DECTCAM_TEST_PATTERN
    bool "Send a synthetic test pattern instead of camera frames"
    default n
    help
      Emits a generated JPEG without touching the camera. Useful for bringing
      up the radio link before the camera is wired, and for isolating camera
      faults from link faults.

config DECTCAM_BENCH_UART0
    bool "Bench mode: stream out of UART0 instead of the nRF link"
    default n
    help
      Sends the packet stream out of UART0, the console port, so the whole
      camera -> JPEG -> fragment -> protocol chain can be verified with no
      jumper wires connected at all. Point the PC viewer at the ESP32's own
      serial port.

      The console REPL and all logging are disabled in this mode, because log
      text on the same wire would corrupt the binary stream.

      Turn this OFF for the real system.

config DECTCAM_BENCH_UART0_BAUD
    int "Bench mode UART0 baud"
    default 921600
    depends on DECTCAM_BENCH_UART0
    help
      The CH343 bridge copes with this comfortably. Drop to 115200 if a
      capture shows corruption.

endmenu
